    <h1>The Raven in Celtic Mythology</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Ireland, Wales, and the battlefield bird</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In Celtic tradition, the raven is primarily a <strong>battle and prophecy bird</strong>. In Irish mythology, the Morrígan — a goddess of fate, battle, and sovereignty — could take crow or raven form. In Welsh mythology, the hero Brân bears a name meaning "raven/crow." Ravens appear at key moments of death and prophecy in the texts. This is a tradition of power and prophetic force, not simple evil.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Morrígan: Ireland's Raven Goddess</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Morrígan (<em>Mór Ríoghain</em>, roughly "great queen" or "phantom queen") is one of the most complex figures in Irish mythology. She is a shape-shifter associated with battle, fate, and the land:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>She appears in the Ulster Cycle, the Mythological Cycle, and the Battle of Mag Tuired</li>
      <li>Her bird form is typically described as a crow or raven (<em>badb, feannóg, fiach</em>)</li>
      <li>In the Ulster Cycle, she attempts to seduce Cú Chulainn and, when rejected, attacks him in various animal forms including an eel, a heifer, and a wolf</li>
      <li>At Cú Chulainn's death, she perches on his shoulder in bird form — the raven on the shoulder confirms the hero is truly dead</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The Morrígan is sometimes described as a triple goddess (with Badb and Macha or Nemain as her aspects — the collective sometimes called the Badb Catha, "battle crow"). The boundaries between crow and raven, and between the goddesses' individual forms, are not rigidly defined in the texts.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Key Textual Appearances</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><em>Táin Bó Cúailnge</em> (Ulster Cycle):</strong> The Morrígan in bird form is central to Cú Chulainn's story — both before and at his death. The raven/crow on the shoulder is the signal that the hero has fallen.</li>
      <li><strong>Second Battle of Mag Tuired (<em>Cath Maige Tuired</em>):</strong> Ravens feature in the battle poetry; the Badb prophesies the battle's outcome in bird-associated imagery.</li>
      <li><strong><em>Fled Dúin na nGéd</em>:</strong> Ravens as battle omens appear in later Irish saga material.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The core pattern across Irish sources: ravens and crow-like birds appear at decisive battles and deaths. Their presence confirms (rather than predicts or causes) doom — they are witnesses and signals, not agents.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Wales: Brân and the Raven Name</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In Welsh tradition, the raven connection operates differently — through personal names and heraldry rather than shapeshifting goddesses:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brân Fendigaid</strong> ("Brân the Blessed") in the Mabinogion: Brân is a giant king of Britain; his name means "raven" or "crow" in Welsh (<em>brân</em> = crow/raven)</li>
      <li>His severed head, buried at the White Mount (traditionally London), is said to protect Britain — this is sometimes connected to the Tower of London raven legend, though that connection is a modern folk etymology, not a medieval source</li>
      <li>Ravens appear in Welsh heraldry and in Arthurian tradition (King Arthur was said in some Welsh traditions to have been transformed into a raven after death)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The "Arthur as raven" belief is documented in folklore but not in the core Welsh Arthurian texts; it was recorded as a popular belief by writers from the 16th century onward.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a>,<a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Gaulish and Continental Celtic Traditions</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Evidence for raven symbolism in Continental Celtic (Gaulish) tradition is more fragmentary:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nantosuelta:</strong> A Gaulish goddess sometimes depicted with a raven on a pole or staff; associated with prosperity and the domestic sphere — a different register entirely from battlefield ravens</li>
      <li><strong>Epona:</strong> Horse goddess; ravens appear in some depictions in secondary roles</li>
      <li>Archaeological corvid deposits at Celtic shrine sites suggest ritual significance of corvids across the Celtic world, though interpretation is contested</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Continental Celtic raven symbolism is less well documented than Irish and Welsh material and must be treated with caution; much depends on interpretation of iconography without textual support.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Crow vs Raven in the Sources</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> A precision point: Old Irish and medieval Welsh texts do not always clearly distinguish between ravens and crows. The terms:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Old Irish <em>fiach</em> (raven), <em>badb/feannóg</em> (crow, hooded crow)</li>
      <li>Welsh <em>brân</em> (crow or raven — used for both)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Modern translations sometimes render the battle-bird as "raven" for dramatic effect when the text may say "crow." Both species were present at battlefields and both would have been familiar to audiences. The symbolic function — a black bird of prophetic power at scenes of death — applies to both, and the texts often treat them as a category rather than distinguishing species precisely.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

